<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day27" xml:id="day-27-lecture">
    <title>Day 27</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 10.1: Neural Nets
        </p>
    </introduction>
<objectives>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>Describe the architecture of a simple feed-forward neural network.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Explain conceptually and mathematically what happens at each layer, including the input, hidden, and output layers.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Explain activation and identify different types of activation functions.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Describe activation mathematically.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Given example inputs and weights from the previous matrix, calculate the activation <m>A</m> by hand, with the help of a calculator.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Explain how a simple feed-forward neural network produces an output.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Given a <m>\beta</m> matrix and the activation from the previous layer, calculate the output by hand.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Identify what the fitted parameters minimize in a simple feed-forward neural network.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

<paragraphs>
  <title>The Idea</title>
      <p><ul >
        <li><p>We have input vector of <m>p</m> variables, <m>X=(X_1,\ldots,X_p)</m>;</p></li>
        <li><p>and some <sq>black-box</sq> nonlinear function <m>f(X)</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>The predicted response <m>\widehat{Y}</m> is the output.</p></li>
        <li><p>We have encountered nonlinear methods before, such as trees.</p></li>
        <li><p>The difference here is the structure of the model.</p></li>
      </ul></p>

</paragraphs>


<paragraphs>
  <title>Feed-Forward Neural Network: The Cartoon</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/Fig10-1.png">
      <shortdescription>Feed-forward neural network diagram.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p><ul >
        <li><p>The input layer consists of the starting data <m>X_i</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>The hidden layer receives inputs from the input layer and converts them into activations <m>A_k</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Each arrow represents taking a linear combination of the values, using weights, to obtain the next value.</p></li>
        <li><p>This network has one hidden layer followed by the output layer.</p></li>
        <li><p>We choose the number of hidden units <m>K</m>; here, <m>K=5</m>.</p></li>
      </ul></p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>A Very Simple Example: Computing <m>A_k</m> for <m>(1,0)</m></title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
    <p>
      <md>
  A_k = h_k(X) = g(w_{k0} + \sum_{j=1}^p w_{kj} X_j)
</md>
</p>
      <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex.jpg">
        <shortdescription>Simple neural network example.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p><ul >
        <li><p><m>A_k</m> are called activations.</p></li>
        <li><p>Choose an activation function <m>g(z)</m>.</p></li>
      </ul></p>
      <p><ul >
        <li><p></p>
          <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex-Input10.jpg">
            <shortdescription>Input values for the example with input (1,0).</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex-Input10-Soln.jpg">
            <shortdescription>Activation calculations for the example with input (1,0).</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </li>
      </ul></p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>A Very Simple Example: Computing <m>Y</m> for <m>(1,0)</m></title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p><m>f(X)=\beta_0+\sum_{k=1}^{K}\beta_k A_k</m></p>
      <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex.jpg">
        <shortdescription>Simple neural network example.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p><ul >
        <li><p></p>
          <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex-Input10.jpg">
            <shortdescription>Input values for the example with input (1,0).</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex-Input10-Soln.jpg">
            <shortdescription>Activation calculations for the example with input (1,0).</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </li>
      </ul></p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>A Very Simple Example: Computing <m>Y</m> for <m>(0,1)</m></title>

    <p>
      <md>
  A_k = h_k(X) = g(w_{k0} + \sum_{j=1}^p w_{kj} X_j)
</md>
</p>
    <p>
    <m>f(X)=\beta_0+\sum_{k=1}^{K}\beta_k A_k</m>
  </p>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex.jpg">
      <shortdescription>Simple neural network example.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p><ul >
        <li>
          <p>For input <m>(0,1)</m>, show the corresponding input values and activation calculations.</p>
          <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex-Input01.jpg">
            <shortdescription>Input values for the example with input (0,1).</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex-Input01-Soln.jpg">
            <shortdescription>Activation calculations for the example with input (0,1).</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </li>
      </ul></p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>A Different Example</title>

      <p>Consider a neural network with input data points having <m>p=3</m>, that is, <m>(X_1,X_2,X_3)</m>, and two units in the hidden layer.</p>
      <p><ul>
        <li>
          <p>Draw the architecture of this neural network.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Using the <m>\omega</m> and <m>\beta</m> matrices, determine the predicted output <m>\widehat{Y}</m> for the input point <m>(2,0,1)</m>.</p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \omega =
              \begin{pmatrix}
              1 &amp; 0 &amp; -2 &amp; 2\\
              -3 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; -1
              \end{pmatrix}
              \qquad
              \beta =
              \begin{pmatrix}
              -1\\
              -2\\
              1
              \end{pmatrix}
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Use the activation function</p>
          <p>
            <md>
              g(z)=(z)_+=
              \begin{cases}
              0 &amp; \text{if } z&lt;0,\\
              z &amp; \text{otherwise}.
              \end{cases}
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul></p>
<image source="figures/NeuralNetwork_example.jpg">
  <shortdescription>Neural network example.</shortdescription>
</image>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>What If There's No Activation Function?</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/NN-Example2-Architecture.jpeg">
        <shortdescription>Architecture of the second neural network example.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        <md>
          \omega =
          \begin{pmatrix}
          1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3\\
          4 &amp; 5 &amp; 6
          \end{pmatrix}
          \qquad
          \beta =
          \begin{pmatrix}
          -1\\
          -2\\
          1
          \end{pmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p><ul >
        <li>
          <p>Calculate the network output with no activation function to show that the result is simply another linear function.</p>
          <image source="figures/NN-Example2-Calc.jpeg">
            <shortdescription>Calculation showing the neural network output without an activation function.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </li>
      </ul></p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Choices for Activation Functions</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p>Sigmoid:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          g(z)=\frac{e^z}{1+e^z}=\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}
        </md>
      </p>
      <image source="figures/Sigmoid-pytorch.png">
        <shortdescription>Sigmoid activation function.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>ReLU: Rectified Linear Unit</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          g(z)=(z)_+=
          \begin{cases}
          0 &amp; \text{if } z&lt;0,\\
          z &amp; \text{otherwise}.
          \end{cases}
        </md>
      </p>
      <image source="figures/ReLU-pytorch.png">
        <shortdescription>ReLU activation function.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Matrix Version: First Layer</title>
  <sidebyside widths="30% 70%">
    <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex.jpg">
      <shortdescription>Simple neural network example.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
    <p>
      <md>
  A_k = h_k(X) = g(w_{k0} + \sum_{j=1}^p w_{kj} X_j)
</md>
</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          A=g(\mathbf{W}\cdot\mathbf{X})
          \qquad
          \mathbf{X}^T=(1\;X_1\;X_2\;\cdots\;X_p)
        </md>
      </p>
      <p><ul >
        <li><p>Calculate the matrix multiplication using the matrices shown on the left to demonstrate that the matrix formulation gives the same equations.</p></li>
      </ul></p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Matrix Version: Output</title>
  <sidebyside widths="30% 70%">
    <image source="figures/DL-simple-ex.jpg">
      <shortdescription>Simple neural network example.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p><m>f(X)=\beta_0+\sum_{k=1}^{K}\beta_k A_k</m></p>
      <p>
        <md>
          Y=\beta\cdot\mathbf{A}
          \qquad
          \mathbf{A}^T=(1\;A_1\;A_2\;\cdots\;A_K)
        </md>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Now What?</title>
  <p>Choose the model parameters by minimizing the residual sum of squares (RSS):</p>
  <p>
    <md>
      \text{RSS}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_i-f(x_i))^2
    </md>
  </p>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p><em>Chosen in advance:</em></p>
      <p><ul>
        <li><p>Number of layers (more on that next lecture).</p></li>
        <li><p>Number of hidden units.</p></li>
        <li><p>Activation function <m>g(z)</m>.</p></li>
      </ul></p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p><em>Tuned by the model:</em></p>
      <p><ul >
        <li><p><m>\omega</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>\beta</m></p></li>
      </ul></p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>


</handout>